bib7"><label>7</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Razi</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Futter</surname><given-names>C.E.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Distinct roles for Tsg101 and Hrs in multivesicular body formation and inward vesiculation</article-title><source>Mol Biol Cell</source><volume>17</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>3469</fpage><lpage>3483</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16707569</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib8"><label>8</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Doyotte</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Russell</surname><given-names>M.R.G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hopkins</surname><given-names>C.R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Woodman</surname><given-names>P.G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Depletion of TSG101 forms a mammalian &#x02018;Class E&#x02019; compartment: a multicisternal early endosome with multiple sorting defects</article-title><source>J Cell Sci</source><volume>118</volume><year>2005</year><fpage>3003</fpage><lpage>3017</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16014378</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib9"><label>9</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nickerson</surname><given-names>D.P.</given-names></name><name><surname>West</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Odorizzi</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Did2 coordinates Vps4-mediated dissociation of ESCRT-III from endosomes</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>175</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>715</fpage><lpage>720</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17130288</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>White</surname><given-names>I.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bailey</surname><given-names>L.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Aghakhani</surname><given-names>M.R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>S.E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Futter</surname><given-names>C.E.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>EGF stimulates annexin 1-dependent inward vesiculation in a multivesicular endosome subpopulation</article-title><source>EMBO J</source><volume>25</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>12</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16052208</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>The authors show that EGF stimulation promotes intralumenal vesicle formation in a manner dependent on the EGFR kinase substrate, annexin 1. Annexin 1 is not required for sorting of cargo, indicating a direct role in EGF-stimulated intralumenal vesicle formation.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib11"><label>11</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shim</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kimpler</surname><given-names>L.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hanson</surname><given-names>P.I.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Structure/function analysis of four core ESCRT-III proteins reveals common regulatory role for extreme C-terminal domain</article-title><source>Traffic</source><volume>8</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>1068</fpage><lpage>1079</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17547705</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Muziol</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pineda-Molina</surname><given-names>E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ravelli</surname><given-names>R.B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zamborlini</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Usami</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gottlinger</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Weissenhorn</surname><given-names>W.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Structural basis for budding by the ESCRT-III factor CHMP3</article-title><source>Dev Cell</source><volume>10</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>821</fpage><lpage>830</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16740483</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>The authors report the crystal structure of CHMP3. They show that the core structure folds into a flat helical arrangement that assembles into a lattice exposing a highly basic membrane binding surface, providing a structural basis for models of how ESCRTIII might promote intralumenal vesicle formation.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib13"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hanson</surname><given-names>P.I.</given-names></name><name><surname>Roth</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Heuser</surname><given-names>J.E.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Plasma membrane deformation by circular arrays of ESCRT-III protein filaments</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>180</volume><year>2008</year><fpage>389</fpage><lpage>402</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18209100</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>The authors show that the CHMP4A and CHMP4B when overexpressed assemble into curved filaments that self-associate in circular arrays that tighten and bend the plasma membrane away from the cytoplasm in the presence of dominant negative VPs4 or when the regulatory C-terminal domain is missing. This shows that a component of the ESCRT machinery can cause the kind of membrane deformation required for intralumenal vesicle formation.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib14"><label>14</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bache</surname><given-names>K.G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Stuffers</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Malerod</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Slagsvold</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Raiborg</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lechardeur</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Walchli</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lukacs</surname><given-names>G.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Brech</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Stenmark</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The ESCRT-III subunit hVps24 is required for degradation but not silencing of the epidermal growth factor receptor</article-title><source>Mol Biol Cell</source><volume>17</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>2513</fpage><lpage>2523</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16554368</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><label>15</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sachse</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Strous</surname><given-names>G.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Klumperman</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>ATPase-deficient hVPS4 impairs formation of internal endosomal vesicles and stabilizes bilayered clathrin coats on endosomal vacuoles</article-title><source>J Cell Sci</source><volume>117</volume><year>2004</year><fpage>1699</fpage><lpage>1708</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15075231</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><label>16</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Matsuo</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Chevallier</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mayran</sur