urname>White</surname><given-names>I.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Annexins and endocytosis</article-title><source>Traffic</source><volume>8</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>951</fpage><lpage>958</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17547702</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><label>26</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Theos</surname><given-names>A.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Truschel</surname><given-names>S.T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tenza</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hurbain</surname><given-names>I.</given-names></name><name><surname>Harper</surname><given-names>D.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Berson</surname><given-names>J.F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Thomas</surname><given-names>P.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Raposo</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Marks</surname><given-names>M.S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A lumenal domain-dependent pathway for sorting to intralumenal vesicles of multivesicular endosomes involved in organelle morphogenesis</article-title><source>Dev Cell</source><volume>10</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>343</fpage><lpage>354</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16516837</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><label>27</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bonifacino</surname><given-names>J.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Rojas</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Retrograde transport from endosomes to the trans-Golgi network</article-title><source>Nat Rev Mol Cell Biol</source><volume>7</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>568</fpage><lpage>579</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16936697</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mari</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bujny</surname><given-names>M.V.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zeuschner</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Geerts</surname><given-names>W.J.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Griffith</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Petersen</surname><given-names>C.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cullen</surname><given-names>P.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Klumperman</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Geuze</surname><given-names>H.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>SNX1 defines an early endosomal recycling exit for sortilin and mannose 6-phosphate receptors</article-title><source>Traffic</source><volume>9</volume><year>2008</year><fpage>380</fpage><lpage>393</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18088323</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>The authors show that MPR and sortilin localise to tubular carriers (ETC) that are morphologically distinct from other carriers involved in recycling that emanate from the same vacuole. Depletion of SNX1 reduced the number of ETCs and the amount of sortilin in the TGN. This study, thus, defines a new class of SNX1-dependent recycling compartment.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib29"><label>29</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Carroll</surname><given-names>K.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hanna</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Simon</surname><given-names>I.</given-names></name><name><surname>Krise</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Barbero</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pfeffer</surname><given-names>S.R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Role of Rab9 GTPase in facilitating receptor recruitment by TIP47</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>292</volume><year>2001</year><fpage>1373</fpage><lpage>1376</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11359012</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><label>30</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Carlton</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bujny</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Peter</surname><given-names>B.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Oorschot</surname><given-names>V.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Rutherford</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mellor</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Klumperman</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>McMahon</surname><given-names>H.T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cullen</surname><given-names>P.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sorting nexin-1 mediates tubular endosome-to-TGN transport through coincidence sensing of high-curvature membranes and 3-phosphoinositides</article-title><source>Curr Biol</source><volume>14</volume><year>2004</year><fpage>1791</fpage><lpage>1800</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15498486</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><label>31</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Arighi</surname><given-names>C.N.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hartnell</surname><given-names>L.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Aguilar</surname><given-names>R.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Haft</surname><given-names>C.R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bonifacino</surname><given-names>J.S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Role of the mammalian retromer in sorting of the cation-independent mannose 6-phosphate receptor</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>165</volume><year>2004</year><fpage>123</fpage><lpage>133</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15078903</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><label>32</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Seaman</surname><given-names>M.N.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cargo-selective endosomal sorting for retrieval to the Golgi requires retromer</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>165</volume><year>2004</year><fpage>111</fpage><lpage>122</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15078902</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><label>33</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Popoff</surname><given-names>V.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mardones</surname><given-names>G.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tenza</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Rojas</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lamaze</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bonifacino</surname><given-names>J.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Raposo</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Johannes</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The retromer complex and clathrin define an early endosomal retrograde exit site</article-title><source>J Cell Sci</source><volume>120</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>2022</fpage><lpage>2031</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17550971</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><label>34</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hierro</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Rojas</surname><given-names>A.L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Rojas</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Murthy</surname><given-names>N.</given-names></name><name><surname>Effantin</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kajava</surname><given-names>A.V.</given-names></name><name><surname>Steven</surname><given-names>A.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bonifacino</surname><given-names>J.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hurley</surname><given-names>J.H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Functional architecture of the retromer cargo-recognition complex</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>449</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>1063</fpage><lpage>1067</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17891154</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><label>35</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Naslavsky</surname><given-names>N.</given-names></name><name><surname>Caplan</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>C-terminal EH-domain-containing proteins: consensus for a role in endocytic trafficking, EH?</article-title><source>J Cell Sci</source><volume>118</volume><year>2005</year><fpage>4093</fpage><lpage>4101</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16155252</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gokool</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tattersall</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Seaman</surname><given-names>M.N.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>EHD1 interacts with retromer to stabilize SNX1 tubules and facilitate endosome-to-golgi retrieval</article-title><source>Traffic</source><volume>8</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>1873</fpage><lpage>1886</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17868075</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>The authors show that EHD1 is upregulated in cells depleted of retromer, partially co-localises with retromer on endosome-derived tubules, and is important for maintaining tubule integrity and efficient recycling of MPR. Mutations in the nucleotide-binding P loop disrupt endosome&#x02013;TGN trafficking, suggesting an essential role for EHD1 ATPase activity.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib37"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Daumke</surname><given-names>O.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lundmark</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Vallis</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Martens</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Butler</surname><given-names>P.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>McMahon</surname><given-names>H.T.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Architectural and mechanistic insights into an EHD ATPase involved in membrane remodelling</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>449</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>923</fpage><lpage>927</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17914359</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>Structural and functional analysis of EHD2 demonstrates how EHD family members may participate in nucleotide-based membrane remodelling. Nucleotide binding generates a curved EHD dimer interface that can bind membrane and induces further oligomerisation. Combined, these processes could contribute to membrane tubulation and perhaps scission.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib38"><label>38</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Peden</surname><given-names>A.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Oorschot</surname><given-names>V.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hesser</surname><given-names>B.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Austin</surname><given-names>C.D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Scheller</surname><given-names>R.H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Klumperman</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Localization of the AP-3 adaptor complex defines a novel endosomal exit site for lysosomal membrane proteins</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>164</volume><year>2004</year><fpage>1065</fpage><lpage>1076</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15051738</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib39"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Traer</surname><given-names>C.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Rutherford</surname><given-names>A.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Palmer</surname><given-names>K.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Wassmer</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Oakley</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Attar</surname><given-names>N.</given-names></name><name><surname>Carlton</surname><given-names>J.G.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kremerskothen</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Stephens</surname><given-names>D.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cullen</surname><given-names>P.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>SNX4 coordinates endosomal sorting of TfnR with dynein-mediated transport into the endocytic recycling compartment</article-title><source>Nat Cell Biol</source><volume>9</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>1370</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17994011</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>Characterisation of SNX4, a PX and BAR domain protein, which localises to endosomal vacuoles, and to associated tubules that lack EGFR and which are distinct from SNX1 tubules. Loss of SNX4 impairs TfR sorting. It is shown that SNX4 interacts with Kibra, a binding partner for the dynein light chain, LC8.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib40"><label>40</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>S.X.</given-names></name><name><surname>Grant</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hirsh</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Maxfield</surname><given-names>F.R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Rme-1 regulates the distribution and function of the endocytic recycling compartment in mammalian cells</article-title><source>Nat Cell Biol</source><volume>3</volume><year>2001</year><fpage>567</fpage><lpage>572</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11389441</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib41"><label>41</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nielsen</surname><given-names>E.</given-names></name><name><surname>Severin</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Backer</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hyman</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zerial</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Rab5 regulates motility of early endosomes on microtubules</article-title><source>Nat Cell Biol</source><volume>1</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>376</fpage><lpage>382</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10559966</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib42"><label>42</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chibalina</surname><given-names>M.V.</given-names></name><name><surname>Seaman</surname><given-names>M.N.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Miller</surname><given-names>C.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kendrick-Jones</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Buss</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Myosin VI and its interacting protein LMTK2 regulate tubule formation and transport to the endocytic recycling compartment</article-title><source>J Cell Sci</source><volume>120</volume><year>2007</year><fpage>4278</fpage><lpage>4288</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18029400</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib43"><label>43</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Barbero</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bittova</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pfeffer</surname><given-names>S.R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Visualization of Rab9-mediated vesicle transport from endosomes to the trans-Golgi in living cells</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>156</volume><year>2002</year><fpage>511</fpage><lpage>518</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11827983</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib44"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hehnly</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sheff</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Stamnes</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Shiga toxin facilitates its retrograde transport by modifying microtubule dynamics</article-title><source>Mol Biol Cell</source><volume>17</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>4379</fpage><lpage>4389</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16885418</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>The authors show that exposure of cells to Shiga toxin B subunit causes a transient increase in the rate of microtubule assembly and that this facilitates efficient transfer of the toxin subunit from endosomes to the Golgi region. Dynein is required for this translocation.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib45"><label>45</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hoepfner</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Severin</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Cabezas</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Habermann</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Runge</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gillooly</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Stenmark</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zerial</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Modulation of receptor recycling and degradation by the endosomal kinesin KIF16B</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>121</volume><year>2005</year><fpage>437</fpage><lpage>450</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15882625</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib46"><label>46</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Allan</surname><given-names>V.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Protein phosphatase 1 regulates the cytoplasmic dynein-driven formation of endoplasmic reticulum networks in vitro</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>128</volume><year>1995</year><fpage>879</fpage><lpage>891</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7876311</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib47"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Roux</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Uyhazi</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Frost</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>De Camilli</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>GTP-dependent twisting of dynamin implicates constriction and tension in membrane fission</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>441</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>528</fpage><lpage>531</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16648839</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>This is a wonderful study examining the function of dynamin, a GTPase required for releasing endocytic vesicles at the plasma membrane. It shows that dynamin will induce scission of kinesin-coated lipid tubules when these are placed under tension by introducing microtubules. Such a combination of motor-induced tension and scission factors may operate to generate tubular intermediates at the endosome.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib48"><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pal</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Severin</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lommer</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Shevchenko</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zerial</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Huntingtin-HAP40 complex is a novel Rab5 effector that regulates early endosome motility and is up-regulated in Huntington's disease</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><volume>172</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>605</fpage><lpage>618</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16476778</pub-id></element-citation><note><p>Characterisation of Huntingtin (Htt)-associated protein 40 (HAP40), a Rab5 effector that mediates the Rab5-dependent recruitment of Htt to endosomes. HAP40 induces a switch of endosomes from microtubules to actin and thereby reduces endosome motility. HAP40 is upregulated in fibroblasts from patients with Huntington's disease and in a mouse model of Huntington's disease.</p></note></ref><ref id="bib49"><label>49</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gasman</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kalaidzidis</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zerial</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>RhoD regulates endosome dynamics through Diaphanous-related Formin and Src tyrosine kinase</article-title><source>Nat Cell Biol</source><volume>5</volume><year>2003</year><fpage>195</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12577064</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib50"><label>50</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fernandez-Borja</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Janssen</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Verwoerd</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hordijk</surname><given-nam